pipeline:

* train models w/ finetune
* use each model in infer_file to get inference results on file by file basis using knockout set of AR regions
* use average to collect results on channel by channel basis to create average mask maps
* use false_color to create color maps   

Training Data

How the 4 CSVs relate and differ:

assets/train_index_surya_1_0.csv — a different dataset entirely. This is the foundation-model (Surya-1.0) input index, not an AR-segmentation label file:

    Columns: ,path,timestep,present — 345,721 rows at 12-minute cadence, 2011-02-15 to 2019-12-31.
    path points to raw SDO NetCDF files (.nc, e.g. 2011/02/20110215_0000.nc) holding the AIA/HMI channels the model consumes as input.
    present (0/1) just flags whether a valid observation exists at that timestep (309,412 present, 36,309 missing).
    Used as data.train_data_path in config.yaml, feeding Surya's own pretraining/embedding pipeline — it has nothing to do with AR masks. (Its sibling valid_index_surya_1_0.csv, not in your list, plays the matching validation role.)

The three surya-bench-ar-segmentation/*.csv files are the downstream AR-segmentation benchmark's label catalogue, all sharing the schema timestamp, file_path, present, hourly cadence, file_path pointing to .h5 mask files (each containing intersection = PIL map and union_with_intersect = the AR footprint mask, the default training target). Together with a test.csv (not in your list), they form a fixed temporal split over 2010–2019 (test covers 2020–2024) documented in GROUND_TRUTH_EXPLAINED.md:
file 	date coverage 	rows 	present=1
train.csv 	15 Feb – 31 Dec, each year 2010–2019 	74,760 	70,823
validation.csv 	15–31 Jan, each year 2010–2019 	3,672 	3,477
leaky_validation.csv 	1–14 Jan and 1–14 Feb, each year 2010–2019 	6,048 	5,753

These are wired into config.yaml as ar_index_train / ar_index_valid (test.csv likewise, though it isn't shipped locally).

Why leaky_validation.csv is separate and called "leaky": it covers the calendar days immediately adjacent to train.csv's start (Feb 15) and wrapping around validation.csv's window (Jan 15–31) — i.e., Jan 1–14 and Feb 1–14. Although no individual timestamp is duplicated across files (verified — zero overlap), active regions rotate and evolve on multi-day timescales, so samples this close to the training window's boundary are temporally correlated with training data, making it an easier/"leaky" validation set rather than a clean held-out one. validation.csv (mid/late January) is the non-leaky held-out split actually used for model selection.

AR-level catalogues (as opposed to the per-timestamp index CSVs above)

These describe *active regions*, one row per HARP, not one row per frame. None are in git; all live in assets/ (a symlink to shared storage). Regenerate any of them with make_holdout_catalog.py — see "Holdout AR catalogs" in README.md.

    harp_catalog_subset.csv — the raw input, and the only one not derived: hourly HARP bounding-box geometry exported from JSOC hmi.Mharp_720s, 2010-05-13 to 2024-12-31, 1,244,172 rows. Columns timestamp, t_rec_tai, harpnum, lat_fwt, lon_fwt, crpix1/2, crsize1/2, lat/lon_min/max. Missing keywords are the literal string MISSING, so it must be read with na_values=["MISSING"]. The test era (2020-2024) holds 496,759 rows over 2,737 HARPs, of which 2,727 have complete geometry.
    unified_catalog.csv — all four split index CSVs concatenated, tagged by a `source` column. Its `test` segment is content-identical to test.csv (43,848 rows, 41,910 present==1, same file_path values), so either can serve as the overlap source.
    harp_catalog_test_full.csv — all 2,727 test-era HARPs, ranked by peak deprojected area.
    holdout_ar_large_from_test.csv — the 88 of the top 100 test-era ARs that deproject cleanly. This is what the uncertainty/inference work actually evaluates on.
    holdout_ar_large_from_training.csv — the 30 training-era ARs chosen to cost the least data to hold out (no validation/leaky overlap, smallest train overlap). Unlike the test-era file this one required real selection: those ARs sit inside the training window, so holding them out means dropping frames.

    holdout_ar_nonflaring_30_from_test.csv — the non-flaring control set: 30 ARs, 10,000-20,000 million km², each with a NOAA region, at most one flare, that flare below C1.0, and nothing in progress at its peak frame. 27 of the 30 have zero flares outright (recover them by filtering n_flares == 0). Built by annotate_flares.py from the band below; adds 7 flare columns after the canonical 14.
    holdout_ar_nonflaring_band_annotated.csv — all 216 band ARs with the same flare columns, kept so the selection criteria can be changed via annotate_flares.py --from-annotated with no network access. Of the 216: 131 have a NOAA region, 85 do not; 112 have zero attributed flares, but only 27 both have a region and are flare-free.
    hek_flares_2020-2024.csv — 95,620 distinct HEK flare events over 2020-2024, cached in 5-day chunks (~349 requests, ~12 min). Reusable for any era or catalog; rebuild with --refresh-cache.

Both holdout_ar_large_* files share one schema, differing only in the trailing <split>_overlap_files column. Ranks are non-contiguous in both because rank is assigned over every AR in the era *before* the deproj/holdout filters — holdout_ar_large_from_test.csv starts at rank 2 because rank 1 (HARP 9475) is a polar limb region tagged deproj: Poor.

Optimizer divergence in the 2016hr regime (2026-10-27)

The first 2016hr member (boot01, run 2026-10-26, wandb f5ksovav, log /efs/bathomas/run_test_2016.log)
diverged. It is neither over- nor undertrained: train and validation loss move together the whole
way, so there is no overfitting gap, and the model had already learned the task after one epoch.

epoch  train loss   val loss   val IoU   pred AR frac (true 0.0064)
  0      0.0111      0.0088    0.5672    0.0074
  1     10.01        0.8355    0.1086    0.0014
  2      0.582       0.2782    0.0793    0.0006
  3      0.0876      0.0414    0.3656    0.0057
  4      1.292       1.2782    0.1071    0.0031

Training BCE stayed under 0.75 for all of epoch 0, then exploded at epoch 1 batch ~685 to a peak
of 930.25 -- saturated wrong-sign logits -- with 33 batches in that epoch over loss 5. Epochs 2-3
partially recovered, epoch 4 blew up again (peak 134.75).

Cause: finetune.py ran Adam at a constant lr 1e-3 with no warmup, no decay and no gradient
clipping, at batch_size 1 against a 0.64%-positive target. optimizer.warm_up_steps and
optimizer.min_lr were present in every config but never read by the code. Step count per epoch is
what tipped it over -- the same lr 1e-3 was stable at 168 steps/epoch (val loss 0.012-0.016
throughout, IoU climbing monotonically), spiky at 672 (run_672.log has an epoch at val loss 121.1,
IoU 0.0000), and chronically divergent at 2016.

Fixed in finetune.py by three opt-in optimizer config keys, so the 168hrs and 672hr configs keep
the constant-LR behaviour their published results were produced under:
  optimizer.lr_schedule     "constant" (default) | "cosine" -- warmup then cosine to min_lr
  optimizer.warm_up_steps   now actually honored, under lr_schedule: cosine
  optimizer.grad_clip_norm  max_norm for clip_grad_norm_; absent/0 disables (the old behaviour)
finetune.py also now writes best.pth per member (best validation IoU) and logs lr and the pre-clip
grad norm, which is the series that would have shown this coming before the loss blew up.

The 2016hr configs were regenerated at lr 2e-4, cosine, 200 warmup steps, grad_clip_norm 1.0 --
see the invocation in make_bootstrap_configs.py's docstring. Regenerating also fixed drift between
members: boot01 had max_epochs 5 and path_experiment under /mnt/efs while boot02-10 had max_epochs
3 and /efs, so the ten were not comparable. All ten now differ only in job_id, path_experiment and
ar_index_train. Pre-regeneration copies are in configs_bootstrap/2016hr.pre-lrfix.20261027/.

Checkpoint status: of /efs/bathomas/ar_seg_bootstrap/2016hr/boot01/epoch_{0..4}.pth, ONLY epoch_0
is usable (IoU 0.5672). epoch_1 through epoch_4 are diverged and epoch_4 -- the one a "use the last
epoch" convention would pick -- predicts less than half the AR area it should. There is no 2016hr
entry in run_bootstrap_inference.py's BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME yet; add one only after a member trains
cleanly under the new settings. Note that the 672hr comment in that table already recorded the same
symptom ("most members then collapse hard") without naming the cause.

672hr_stable: the stabilized 672hr ensemble (created 2026-10-27)

configs_bootstrap/672hr_stable/ + run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable.sh. The old 672hr set is
untouched and still runnable; this is an additional set, not a replacement.

Why: 8 of the 10 original 672hr members collapse after their peak. Best epochs land at 0-3 of 5
(mean 1.5), so 60-70% of the compute was wasted or harmful:

  boot02  ep1 0.5608 -> ep4 0.0000   (-0.561)
  boot06  ep1 0.6089 -> ep4 0.0572   (-0.552)
  boot03  ep2 0.6169 -> ep4 0.3177   (-0.423)
  boot01  ep2 0.6036 -> ep9 0.4169   (-0.375)
  boot09  ep1 0.5621 -> ep4 0.2720   (-0.341)
  boot04/08/10                       (-0.25 to -0.28)

3.91% of 672hr training batches exceed loss 1.0 and 0.43% exceed 100 (max 655.84) -- the same
divergence as 2016hr, milder. For contrast, 168hr's max training loss over 6,660 batches is 0.75
and *no* batch exceeds 1.0, which is why 168hr was left alone: it is data-limited, not
optimizer-limited (boot01 ran 20 epochs and peaked at epoch 8 with 0.5170, mid-pack among the
10-epoch members).

What changed from 672hr, and only this:
  warm_up_steps  0    -> 70  (~10% of one epoch)
  lr_schedule    -    -> cosine        (warm_up_steps is only honored under cosine)
  grad_clip_norm -    -> 1.0
  max_epochs     5    -> 10            (boot01 was already 10; the drift is normalized)
learning_rate stays 1e-3 deliberately. 672hr reaches the highest IoU of any regime (0.6341)
despite the instability, so the LR magnitude is productive; clipping is the targeted fix for
occasional blow-ups and does not cost convergence speed the way dropping to 2e-4 would.

NOTE ON WHAT IS DOING THE WORK: cosine leaves the LR at 9.8e-4 / 9.1e-4 during epochs 1-2, which
is where the old members collapsed -- effectively the old flat 1e-3. So **grad clipping alone is
what has to prevent those collapses.** If they recur, the schedule is not the reason and lowering
learning_rate is the next lever, not a longer warmup.

The replicate indices, evaluation indices, LoRA rank/alpha, workers and dtype are identical to
the old 672hr set, and checkpoints go to .../ar_seg_bootstrap/672hr_stable/bootNN, so this is a
clean A/B on stability alone.

run_bootstrap_inference.py's regime_defaults() already resolves "672hr_stable" correctly by
naming convention, but --regime is gated on BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME's keys, so inference will
refuse the regime until a real entry is added from the run's output.log.

Cost: 672 samples x ~590 MB = ~396 GB/epoch, ~4.0 TB/member, ~40 TB for all 10 at 10 epochs.
Regenerate with --max-epochs 5 for the ~20 TB version.

CAVEAT: if this ensemble supersedes the old 672hr one, the sections 6/7 analysis (ICC, reliability,
the 2.28x error/uncertainty ratio, "member diversity is lost") has to be redone. Some of those
conclusions may be artifacts of members collapsing at different random epochs rather than real
consequences of 4x the training data.
